AST structure
-------------
* Decl - roughly Stmt or Expr in our parser.
* DeclContext - some decls can act as declaration contexts - scope where things can be declared and looked up (possibly by name). For example, the 'file' is TranslationUnit context, but several other decl types inherit from DeclContext: namespaces, classes, functions, the body of a `requires` expression, capture statements of lambdas, 
* NamedDecl - *may* have a name - 



Aside: declarators
------------------
A declarator is the part of a declaration that declares a name and describe *what* that names is (pointer, reference, array, function, etc). The basic type is not part of the declarator - it is instead
usually part of the declaration specifier.


* Declaration specifiers may say:
(int, static, constexpr, typedef, etc.)
-> the basic type is int, and these are the "things that an int can be", that the thing being declared here is.

* Declarator says what the name is, and what it is in relation to the basic type.

Examples

int *p[10];
- Declaration specifiers - basic type is int
- Declarator: *p[10] - name is p, and it's an array of pointers (to the basic type)


Eli Bendersky talks about this a bit a post (http://eli.thegreenplace.net/2012/07/05/how-clang-handles-the-type-variable-name-ambiguity-of-cc) about clang circa 2012.

"To follow this process through, we'll start in Parser::ParseDeclarationSpecifiers [1]. In the C/C++ grammar, type names are part of the "specifiers" in a declaration (that also include things like extern or inline)"
Eli also mentions how all names in C are lexically scoped, but "C++ makes this type lookup problem much more difficult by forcing declarations inside a class to be visible throughout the class, even in code that appears before them".

```
int aa(int arg) {
    return arg;
}

class C {
    int foo(int bb) {
        return (aa)(bb);     // this is not a call to aa, rather a cast of bb to int
    }

    typedef int aa;
};
```

* "When parsing an inline member function declaration/definition, it does full parsing and semantic analysis of the declaration, leaving the definition for later" -> perhaps we can encapsulate contexts purely, and add
the parsing of inline member function definitions to a 'parse job queue'. Actually - just parsing after the class declaration is parsed is enough (rather than doing multiple passes).

* Eli also briefly mentions how the Sema module interacts with the parser, maintaining the type table etc
and the parser interacts with Sema via APIs like Sema::getTypeName (whether an identifier is a type).

i.e in Clang, the parser incrementally interacts with the Sema library, which builds the AST.
See https://bcain-llvm.readthedocs.io/projects/clang/en/latest/InternalsManual/#sema

Takeaways
----------
At the Parser layer, we can keep representing types (including templated types and 'possibly a type' things like Foo<bar>::x) as TypeParse. The important thing is to keep the qualified name representation around, so that when we do semantic analysis, we perform direct lookup within a declaration context with the qualified name. If the qualified name is `::b::foo<x>` it's potentially different from `b::foo::<x>` r `foo:<x>`.

We may need a more flexible type representation *at the Sema layer* - e.g. PointerType vs ArrayType vs RecordType that can all be a Type, but that need not leak into Parser.